  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nDry and cool surface high pressure will be the rule over the\nQuad State through Monday, while a skinny ridge aloft lingers\noverhead. However, mid-level energy may sneak through the ridge\nand bring some sprinkles into southern Illinois this morning, \nand again tonight into early Sunday over the western half of the\nregion.\n\nSouth winds will return Tuesday as an upper trough pushes east \nthrough the northern Plains and Upper Midwest. This will\nflatten the upper ridge, resulting in southwest flow aloft for \nthe remainder of the work week. The south winds at the surface\nwill allow temperatures to climb above normal Wednesday through\nat least Friday.\n\nA closed upper low over AZ/NM Wednesday will weaken and become \npositively-tilted as it moves east toward the Quad State in the\nwake of the northern stream system. 20-40% chances of showers \nenter the forecast Wednesday, mainly over southeast Missouri and\nsouthern Illinois. With time, those chances will spread over \nthe entire region Thursday into Friday. At this time, instability\nappears very limited (<500J/kg), so thunderstorms should not be\nprevalent, but the NBM does have some slight chances of thunder in\nthe afternoon over southeast Missouri Thursday and Friday. No \nsevere weather or heavy rainfall is expected at this time.",
